          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Predicting What Comes Next: Exploring Sequences and Progressions"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.845,
            0.068,
            0.883,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "189"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.115,
            0.399,
            0.139
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.153,
            0.855,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe the Sierpiński triangle and try to answer the following questions"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.176,
            0.79,
            0.196
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(a) How many black triangles are there in Stages 0 to 3 of Fig. 8.7?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.199,
            0.804,
            0.219
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(b) Can you predict the number of black triangles at Stages 4 and 5?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.223,
            0.851,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(c) Can you find a rule for the number of black triangles at the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " stage?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.246,
            0.855,
            0.344
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(d) Suppose the area of the triangle (that is, the black region) in Stage 0 is 1 square unit. What is the area of the black region in Stages 1, 2 and 3? What will be the area of the black region in Stages 4 and 5? Find a rule for the area of the black region at the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " stage. What happens to this area as n, the number of stages, goes on increasing?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.362,
            0.886,
            0.462
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We observe that the number of black triangles is 1, 3, 9 and 27 in Stages 0, 1, 2 and 3 respectively. In fact, in every stage, each black triangle is replaced with three smaller triangles at the next stage. Thus, the number of black triangles at every stage is three times the number at the previous stage."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.469,
            0.883,
            0.49
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "At Stages 4 and 5 the number of black triangles will be 81 and 243."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.498,
            0.886,
            0.654
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that the sequence 1, 3, 9, 27, 81, 243, … is a GP since every successive term of the sequence can be obtained by multiplying the previous term by 3. Also, all the terms of this sequence are powers of 3: "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 = 3^{0}, \\; 3 = 3^{1}, \\; 9 = 3^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and so on. Continuing in this manner we observe that the exponent of 3 matches the stage number, as shown in Table 1. Thus, the number of black triangles at the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " stage of the Sierpiński triangle is given by "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 ^ { n }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . The number of black triangles increases very quickly as the stage numbers increase. Can you explain why?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.652,
            0.886,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What about the area of the black region? In Stage 1, the equilateral triangle at Stage 0 is divided into 4 equal parts and the central part is removed. This means that, if the black region at Stage 0 is 1 square unit, then then the black region at Stage 1 is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 3 } { 4 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " square units. This process is repeated on Stage 1 to arrive at Stage 2. Hence, the black region at Stage 2 will be "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{3}{4}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the black region of Stage 1, which is equal to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{3}{4} \\times \\frac{3}{4} = \\left( \\frac{3}{4} \\right)^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Can you explain why the area of the black region at Stage n will be "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\left( { \\frac { 3 } { 4 } } \\right) ^ { n } ?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.881,
            0.886,
            0.923
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The area of the black region at each stage also leads to a geometric progression where every successive term of the sequence is obtained"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
